    Every write re-serializes the whole document.

  [1mC. B plus a defaults registry and migration hook[0m — [38;5;153m{"v":1,"data":{…}}[39m, keys registered up front with their default and
  a version-keyed migration function.
  - Buys: [38;5;153mget()[39m on an unset key returns a real default instead of [38;5;153mundefined[39m; a defined path for changing the stored
    shape later.
  - Costs: a registry contradicts the "no fixed set of keys" decision you just made, and the migration hook is machinery
    for a schema change that hasn't happened. This is the YAGNI option.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m The cross-tab clobbering in B and C is a genuine correctness bug rather than a tradeoff, and
  it's the kind that shows up as "my setting randomly reverted" long after you'd think to look for it. A avoids it
  structurally. The one thing worth taking from C is defaults — but as a [38;5;153mget(key, fallback)[39m argument, which costs
  nothing and needs no registry.

  Two things I'd build into whichever you pick, because they're not optional:
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m can throw[0m — Safari private mode, blocked site data, quota exhaustion. The module probes once and falls
    back to an in-memory [38;5;153mMap[39m, so the app degrades to "settings work but don't persist" instead of throwing inside a
    click handler.
  - [1mNo credentials path.[0m The login form stays unwired from this module, and I won't add a "remember me" or stored
    username — you didn't ask for one, and it's the single most likely way a preferences store turns into a credential
    leak.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Layout [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhich storage layout should I design around?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mA: key per preference (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mprefs:<name> per setting. No cross-tab clobbering, isolated corruption, mirrors the key-value API. Enumeration [39m
     [38;5;246mneeds a prefix scan.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m B: single JSON blob
     [38;5;246mOne key holding all preferences, cached in memory. Atomic multi-set and easy enumeration, but tabs can silently [39m
     [38;5;246moverwrite each other.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m C: versioned blob + defaults registry
     [38;5;246mB plus a registered key list and migration hook. More structure up front; the registry conflicts with the [39m
     [38;5;246mno-fixed-keys decision.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
